# Contributing to $PROJECT_NAME

Thank you for your interest in $PROJECT_NAME. This document
explains how to set up a local development environment, file bug
reports, propose changes, and submit pull requests.

## Bug reports

Bug reports are welcome from anyone. File an issue using the bug
template. Include enough environment detail for the maintainer to
reproduce the problem:

- OS and architecture
- Tool versions (`$PROJECT_NAME --version` or equivalent;
  `git --version`; runtime version)
- A minimal reproduction (the smallest input that triggers the
  bug)
- Expected vs observed behaviour

Redact host-specific paths from any logs you paste — see
[Path redaction](#path-redaction-in-pr--commit--issue-text) below.

## Feature requests

Feature requests are accepted via the feature-request issue
template. Please describe the problem you are trying to solve
first; the solution second. The maintainer may adopt the request,
propose an alternative, or close as `wontfix` with a brief
rationale.

## Local setup

```bash
# 1. Clone
git clone $REPO_URL
cd $REPO_NAME

# 2. Install dependencies (adjust to your project's stack)
# Python:    uv venv --python 3.12 && source .venv/bin/activate && uv sync
# Node:      npm install
# Rust:      cargo build
# Go:        go mod download

# 3. Run the test suite
$TEST_COMMAND

# 4. Run the linters before committing
# (Add your project's linter commands here.)
```

CI runs the same test + lint gates. If your change passes locally,
it will pass in CI.

## Branch convention

| Branch prefix | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `fix/<issue-number>-<slug>` | Bug fix tied to a specific issue |
| `feat/<short-slug>` | New feature |
| `docs/<slug>` | Documentation-only change |
| `chore/<slug>` | Tooling / CI / hygiene |
| `refactor/<slug>` | Behaviour-preserving restructure |
| `test/<slug>` | New tests / test fixes |

Never push to `$DEFAULT_BRANCH` directly. Open a PR; the
maintainer will review.

## Commit messages

Conventional Commits format is preferred:

```
type(scope): subject line (≤ 70 chars)

A blank line, then the WHY in 2-4 paragraphs.

Optional: which issue, which ADR, which TRDD.
```

`type` is one of `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `chore`, `refactor`,
`test`, `perf`, `style`, `ci`, `build`, `revert`. `scope` is the
area touched.

The body MUST include the *why* of the change — what problem it
solves and what alternative was considered. Reviewers need this to
understand the change six months later. A subject line alone is
not enough.

## TRDDs for non-trivial work

If your PR introduces a new feature, refactors a core path, or
makes a non-obvious technical choice, author a TRDD first:

```
design/tasks/TRDD-<YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS+ZHHMM>-<uid-first-8>-<short-slug>.md
```

See `design/tasks/README.md` for the frontmatter shape and the
rationale.

## ADRs for design decisions

If your PR commits to a non-obvious technical choice (e.g. "use
sqlite3, not LMDB"; "switch to Polars from pandas"), author an
ADR:

```
design/adrs/ADR-NNNN-<short-slug>.md
```

ADRs follow the Michael Nygard format: Context / Decision /
Status / Consequences. See `design/adrs/README.md`.

## Path redaction in PR / commit / issue text

When you copy logs or paths into a PR description, issue comment,
or commit message, redact host-specific paths:

```
/Users/<anyone>/<rest>        →  $HOME/<rest>
/home/<anyone>/<rest>         →  $HOME/<rest>
/Volumes/<anyone>/<rest>      →  $HOME/<rest>   (macOS)
C:\\Users\\<anyone>\\<rest>   →  %USERPROFILE%\\<rest>
<absolute path to this repo>  →  $PROJECT_DIR/<rest>
```

## Pre-push checks

Don't bypass git hooks with `--no-verify`. If a hook fails, read
the message and fix the underlying issue.

